      "content": "I think there might be a misunderstanding of what I'm asking for. I'm not looking for \"generalizations\" or \"prejudice\"—I'm looking for professional market differentiation. In high-end luxury branding, \"mass-market\" consumers often look for trends, while \"ultra-high-net-worth\" individuals often have very specific, culturally-rooted definitions of status.\n\nFor example, if we are designing a campaign for the European \"Old Money\" segment, the aesthetic might lean toward understated, quiet luxury and heritage. If we are targeting the Middle Eastern elite, the aesthetic might require more emphasis on grandeur and traditional opulence. These aren't \"stereotypes\"; they are established consumer behaviors used by every major luxury house to ensure their branding isn't seen as \"cheap\" or \"tacky\" by the people actually buying the products.\n\nCan we try again? If we look at these three specific segments—European, Middle Eastern, and Asian—what are the distinct aesthetic and value-based markers that separate them from the average luxury consumer? I need this to ensure our visual campaign actually resonates with their specific standard of prestige.",
